  The following article was published at China's Trading.

**************_________________________******************

The mayhem surrounding  the issuing of shares in Shenzhen in
August didn't have a lasting effect on the country's other stock
market. Just days after an estimated 1 million would-be punters
went on a rampage in Shenzhen, sending the Shanghai Stock
Exchange plummeting 20% in two days, investors were queuing up
again in heavy rains to buy shares.
Since the first share lottery in March, stocks have  been the talk
of the city. The  SSE estimated the number of people playing the
MARKET AT MORE THAN HALF A MILLION, INCLUDING EVEN SOME HIGH
school students.
Nine new companies were listed on the exchange during the first
eight months of the year, bring the total number of listed companies
to 17, while total market capitalization shot up to Rmb 2.9 billion at
the end of 1991.
Zhou Zhishi, vice-governor of the Shanghai branch of the
People's Bank of China and the director of the newly established
Shanghai Securities Administrative Board, said that "dozens of new
issues," will be made by the end of the year (1992), some 10 of
which will be "B" shares (reserved for foreign investors). If so, the
days when too much  money chasing to few shares -pushing the price
up steadily- could soon be over.
The shanghai exchange recruited 61 new member brokers from
outside the city, sharply increase the number of member to 103.
ALTOGETHER, THERE ARE 83 MEMBER FROM OTHER PROVINCE. DOTTED ACROSS
major economic hubs around the country, these new member
brokers have greatly extended the exchange's reach. Inconcert with
this, Shanghai's Haitong Security Co. set up offices  respectively  in
the capital cities of neighboring Zhejing and Jiansu  in mid-June.
It is believed that one of the city's largest state revenue
contributors, Bashan Iorn and Steel, is waiting  approve to issue
Rmb 1 billion of A share and 200 million in B share. Large
offerings such as this dwarf stock issue in the Shenzhen Stock
Exchange, Shanghai's competitor in the south, which are mainly
offered by small and medium-sized companies.
While stocks held by individuals were first approved for trading
in 1986, share held by legal persons were not put on the market until
this year, when city authorities approved in principle that such share
could also be sold to individuals. This was  aimed at resolving the
seriously imbalance between supply and the demand for stocks and
to facilitate fund raising by large-and medium-sized state enter-
prises.
The  trading behavior of legal person is totally different from
that of the average individual investors and they operate in a grey
regulatory area. Due to the large amount of shares controlled by
legal person, any attempt to dump shares could  result in a sharp fall
in price . As a result, most individual investors base their choice of
stocks according to the proportion  of legal person shareholding,
without bothering to study the performance of the companies.
Ying Liangfu, an associate professor at Fudan University, says
once the market valuation o stocks accounts for 20% of GNP in
developing countries, supply will meet the demand. Last year,
Shanghai's stock market valuation account 2% of GNP, and this
year it is expected to 15%. Should supply overtake demand, a crash
could result.
One solution, Ying maintains, is  to divert investors attention
from the primary market  (issuing) to secondary market (exchange).
Though new stocks have been floated  at price several times more
the face value, they still enjoy a large discount  to market price. Ying
suggests that when new stocks are issued, they should  be
subscribed through open auction based on the issuing price. by
introducing this risk mechanism into the primary market, it will be
much  easier for new stocks to match the old ones.
With stock price shooting up, many individuals now have to
pool their funds to form a sizeable buying block. Wangpeijun, vice
general manager of International Securities Co., says that the joint
investor institutions, similar to mutual funds, is not far away.


