Date: Mon, 18 Jan 93 15:22:33 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Re:  PPL: Why Not Let Convicts Run the Police?

I wrote:
> With a private civil law, a complaining victim expects to gain a
> restitution from the guilty criminal, who expects to suffer a punishment.
> This wealth transfer mainly comes from a fine which the criminal owes to
> the victim.  The difference, punishment minus restitution, is the cost of
> the law enforcement...

Mark Pickens responded:
>Once again, we may be using different terminology.  I have always considered
>"restitution" and "punishment" to be completely different concepts.
>Restitution means, to me, the act of restoring the balance between the
>parties to what it would have been if the crime had not taken place.
>Punishment, in contrast, is the infliction of loss on the criminal in a
>manner which does not restore anything to the victim.  

My dictionary does not support your narrow definition of the word
"punishment" (no restriction on victim restoration is mentioned), nor does
it indicate that "restitution" must be full and exact to meaningfully be
restitution ("partial restitution" is not a contradiction in terms).
However, I would be happy to entertain suggestions for alternative
more-neutral substitute terms.

>If we accept these
>definitions, it follows that the only legal systems a Libertarian society
>would consider legitimate are those that produce restitution, NOT
>punishment.  

I was not aware such a formal definition of "Libertarian" was widely
accepted.  In doing an economic analysis of law, I am seeking laws which
(when combined with some direct wealth transfer) would be in the
self-interest of all affected parties, so that they should all endorse a
transition to such a law.  If that makes me not a Libertarian, so be it.
My paper suggests that exact restitution is not always the best law.

Robin Hanson

