I thought this might be interesting enough to post, but I make no claims
that any of these experiences in themselves  have any relevancy to anyone
but myself. Despite some positive results for myself, my extensive
occult/magickal library mostly just gathers dust these days...

I've practiced meditation on and off for some years. In one case use
of relaxation techniques helped me to overcome "panic attacks". I
realize this may not exactly be mediation for it's own sake.

Also, I once achieved a (drug-free!) state of "expanded consciousness"
which lasted for 2 days. Colors were brighter, etc. Marvin Minskys'
comment in _Society of Mind_ (from memory): <One thinks that all questions
are answerable, until one remembers that no questions were asked> may apply
here; at least I can certainly agree with this.

Earlier, I read a book on ritual magic which began with a "proof":
perform a ritual (a request for "proof" of magic) and see what happens.
I did, and saw the results, which convinced me that "magic" was "real".
I consider it significant that I remember I was convinced, but do not
remember the supposed magical "event".

I once tried a telepathy test with my SO. A series of 25 attempts with
5 symbols to choose from. She got exactly 5 right, but 4 of these were
sequential...

I have heard really wierd things from apparently stable conservative
people. A secretary at a previous job related several tales of what
she claimed were poltergeist experiences at her house (glasses
exploding, etc).Beyond this she seemed almost pathetically normal.
My sister at one time heard radio reports from some days in the future.
A friend claimed to astrally project, suddenly and uncontrollably.
I've had interesting experiences following staring at tiny (foreign)
coins close to my eye before sleeping, or, while reclining, focusing 
a spot on the floor across the room. Both of these things caused
spatial/distortion, "pressure" dreams, in one case, I felt I was
"returning to my body", with a pov going slowly down the stairs to
where I was sleeping.

It seems almost everyone has such wierd tales to describe, if not
from their own experience, from the experience of a mysterious aunt
or whatever. RA Wilson claims most doctors have had patients who go
into spontaneous remission, despite official AMA support and a lack
of literature on the subject.

In some sense I get the feeling that children are more open to such
experiences, which is unfortunate because they may lack the ability
to communicate them effectively or to repeat the experience on command.
In any case such experiences may not be reproducable in a laboratory
setting, especially if Mr Randi has sent in agents to skew the data :)

I would hope that "Flying Yogis" doesn't become a metaphor for
everything that can't be observed in clinical settings....

