Date: Mon, 18 Jan 93 15:31:46 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Re:  PPL: Why Not Let Convicts Run the Police?

Mark Pickens wrote:
> Restitution means, to me, the act of restoring the balance between the
> parties to what it would have been if the crime had not taken place.

Eli Brandt responded:
>Hence, in a pure-restitution legal system, the potential
>criminal's disincentive will be 
> (probability of conviction)(cost of restitution), 
>as compared to the incentive of (benefit from crime).  For crimes such
>as theft of cash, the cost of restitution is simply the amount stolen
>plus the prevailing interest rate.  Even a perfect legal system
>couldn't make a pure-restitution policy effective as a deterrent --
>particularly if the thief had a good investment opportunity.

To which Mark responded:
>Does a Libertarian legal system (necessarily based on restitution) provide
>adequate disincentive to criminals?  One condition of living in a particular
>private community could be the requirement of contractual agreement that
>conviction of a crime results in punitive as well as actual damages.  The
>punitive damages could, for example, be assigned to the community to fund
>assistance to uncompensated crime victims.

Before Mark had written:

>Once restitution is complete, the legal job is finished.  (Yes, full
>restitution does include investigation, legal fees and court costs.  The
>criminal could avoid legal and court costs by settling out of court.  He
>could also avoid investigation costs by admitting the crime before
>investigation.)  

If you're willing to include add in investigation, court, etc. costs,
why not also just add in the cost of insuring against the possibility
of not catching and convicting the criminal?  That's what you're
trying to do with your community fund idea, but why socialize it that
much?  Each victim can privately and individually insure against this
risk, and charge it to the criminal if caught.  My paper gives more
detail of how to do this.  

Robin Hanson

