Date: Mon, 18 Jan 93 15:45:24 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Re:  PPL: Why Not Let Convicts Run the Police?

Tom Morrow wrote:
>The caught criminal will, thanks to systems for measuring and
>conveying legal trustworthiness in a PPL, suffer the loss of countless
>opportunities to associate with third parties.  In many circumstances
>(hiring, housing, offering credit, admitting into a private community,
>covering with insurance, etc.) people will choose not to deal with those
>who have criminal records, or demand higher rates to compensate for the
>risks of doing so.  

I don't think it is obvious that we should each prefer a law which
publicizes every verdict.  This information is valuable to the rest of
society, but is the sort of public good which is likely to be
underproduced in a PPL.  Consider that most private arbitration
results are kept private, and that the law of this list apparently
keeps some verdicts private (such as the recently revealed previous
warnings to Perry).  Looking after my self interest, I might rather
negotiate for a higher restitution, should I be a victim, than
insisting that the verdict be publicized.  Similarly, as a potential
criminal, I might rather agree to pay a higher crime that to get my
name trashed over any little offense.

