Date: Mon, 18 Jan 93 16:17:19 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Immortalist conservatism

Tom Morrow writes:
>When a deathist considers a long-term commitment, she must take account of
>the fact that her choice may well constrain the rest of her life.  When she
>chooses a career, for example, she must keep in mind that she may not get a
>chance to try alternative careers before she dies.  She will probably get
>only one opportunity to master a particular field of work, and will thus
>choose careers very conservatively.
>
>An immortalist, on the other hand, can afford to take greater risks with
>regard to long term commitments.  He realizes that he can spend 30 years in
>engineering, rising to the top of his profession, and then go on to start
>anew as a painter if he pleases.  He will thus tend to be less conservative
>in choosing a career.

This argument seems to neglect the basic facts of labor economics.
Why does it now takes ~30 years to rise to the top of a profession
like engineering?  Not because of some instrinsic property of
engineering; rather that is near the upper limit of how long most
people can now devote to rising.  Should people like 700 years instead
of 70, it might take many centuries to rise to the top.

You would still face the difficult choice of staying in a career
you've been in a long time, and so are good at and getting better, vs.
switching and doing something more fun, but at a cost in wages,
prestige, power etc.  Folks now face that choice, and many like me are
willing to pay to switch several times.  But most folk, even wealthy
folk, tend to stay to build up their human capital.

So I predict "immortals" will stay in careers for a *very* long time.

Robin Hanson

