> I don't agree with the notion of implicit consent.  Consider the
> following example:  a sealed package (containing, typically,
> computer software) bears on its outside a license agreement and a
> label saying:  opening of this package constitutes agreement to the
> terms of this license...
 
> Richard Kennaway
 
When you PURCHASED the package you implicitly agreed to the contract, NOT
when you opened it.  A reasonable person would assume that the owner of that
software would not have sold it to you unless you first agreed to abide by
those terms.
 
Do you deny the validity of all assumed agreements?  What if you buy a loaf
of bread from me, eat a slice, then go into convulsions?  After you get out
of the hospital you analyze the bread and determine that it contains
strychnine.  You then sue me for damages.  
 
What is your legal theory?  Are you saying that I knew (or should have
known) that the bread was poisoned; that you wouldn't have purchased it if
you had known that fact?  Granted, but so what?  There's no label on it
stating:  "Does not contain strychnine."  I never said it wasn't poisoned,
you merely assumed that (as would any reasonable person).
 
> If I know that trespassers may be shot on sight...It surely does not
> require that, if caught, I have agreed to be shot and must therefore
> meekly let it happen
 
If we stipulate that you knew that if you trespassed you would be shot and
that you actually did trespass voluntarily, fully understanding the
consequences, when you're shot for doing so, what exactly are your grounds
for complaint?  When you sue for damages, what is your legal theory?  It
would be different if you accidently trespassed, that you didn't know the
rule, or that you didn't understand the rule (say if you were drunk,
retarded, or senile), or if you were forced to trespass, but you're not
saying that.  You're saying that you did it on purpose, with full knowledge
of the consequences of your actions.
 
Must you therefore "meekly let it happen?"  Of course not.  You certainly
CAN resist, just be responsible for any damages you cause by that
resistance.  Remember, you can always break an agreement, just be willing to
accept the consequences.
 
 
